# Task: Script Analysis and Integration

Perform an analysis of the efficiency of the sound module in the [Synthesize script](#view-scripts-synthesize). How much of a hit would the game engine take if it incorporated YAML/data-model driven scores? 

Keep in mind, there will also be premade `.wavs` and `.mp3` loaded and indexed by the engine, over and above the Notes model detailed the script:

```bash
(.venv) grant@skynet:~/Projects/ontology/src/assets/sounds$ ls
main.yaml  music  noises  vocals
(.venv) grant@skynet:~/Projects/ontology/src/assets/sounds$ ls music
arabesque.mp3  dark-amulet.mp3  gran-batalla.mp3  heroic-demise.mp3  kings-crowning.mp3  no-more-magic.mp3  prologue.mp3  soliloquy.mp3
(.venv) grant@skynet:~/Projects/ontology/src/assets/sounds$ ls noises
armor.wav  beads.wav  bottle.wav  bubble.wav  chain.wav  cloth.wav  coin.wav  creak.wav  magic.wav  metal.wav  mud.wav  puddle.wav  spell.wav  splash.wav  splat.wav  swing.wav  unlock.wav  unsheathe.wav  wood.wav
(.venv) grant@skynet:~/Projects/ontology/src/assets/sounds$ ls vocals
bite.wav  blagh.wav  burp.wav  fart.wav  groan.wav  grunt.wav  moan.wav  roar.wav
```

These assets will perform the traditional role of in-game sounds.

The Notes model would be used for interactable instruments in-game, i.e. the Player interacting with a Piano Asset will either pop up a Music Menu, or else switch the Device context so the Player can play the instrument with button-mapped controls. Think Zelda: Ocarina of Time, where playing certain notes in certain orders throws events that are subsequently processed in-game. Where Ontology will differ is the processing of music events will have psychological effects on other Sprites. 

Put together a backlog item using the template for sounds based on your analysis.

